WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
Christopher Riley, Author/Screenwriter: I really&nbsp;
value stories told by writers who are able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:10.160
go out into life and experience it and&nbsp;
come back and help make some sense of it.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:16.960
Film Courage: Can you explain why a writer&nbsp;
must understand their own story before they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.720
essentially understand any character&nbsp;
stories that they may invent?

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:28.320
Chris: My wife Kathy took this idea&nbsp;
of defining moments and pushed it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:37.600
a step further by saying this isn't just about&nbsp;
concocting moments for our characters it really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:45.200
begins with us understanding our own story so she&nbsp;
when she teaches young writers will say again and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.560
again before you can understand your character&nbsp;
story you have to understand your own story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:59.520
so if we're going to understand the dynamic of&nbsp;
the way moments can reshape us and transform us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:07.520
the best place for us to look is in our&nbsp;
own experience we have a front row seat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:17.760
to our own invisible inner emotional life and so&nbsp;
we've got to be willing to to draw on that but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:27.120
before I can draw on my own life my own defining&nbsp;
moments I have to actually locate them so while I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:35.120
might not want to crack open an old dvd I have to&nbsp;
crack open my own history if I'm going to tap into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:45.840
the emotional riches that lie there for all of&nbsp;
us because we all have in the words of author&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.600
Frederick Buechner wonderful and terrible&nbsp;
things that have happened to us all and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:59.680
the idea is that we would look at those things so&nbsp;
as an example a kind of moment that might define&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:08.320
us would be the moment a dream was born and so&nbsp;
somebody who's pursuing acting directing writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:16.800
often that that's based around a dream&nbsp;
and so the question that I would ask is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:24.800
when was that dream born can you locate the&nbsp;
moment of birth for that dream and sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:34.560
there is a moment and sometimes things emerge so&nbsp;
gradually that we can't locate a moment we've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:41.360
glaciers that creep imperceptibly but then&nbsp;
we also have glaciers when they reach the sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.200
that in dramatic fashion will&nbsp;
cave a glacier in a moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:57.360
what we're interested in as dramatists are those&nbsp;
dramatic moments so the tectonic plates of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:04.720
earth move milli meter by millimeter over&nbsp;
the decades centuries millennia it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:12.320
that interesting to watch but there is an instant&nbsp;
when they rupture and we have an earthquake those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:22.160
are the dramatic moments so there are times when&nbsp;
changes happen in us by these gradual processes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:32.000
and that we may not call those defining moments&nbsp;
but there are these cataclysmic seismic moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:41.840
of change that make great stories but also&nbsp;
really help to define how that change happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:50.240
so you might look back and say let me locate&nbsp;
what is that moment where I became obsessed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:56.240
screenwriting when is that moment when I&nbsp;
had to make movies or had to make television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:05.760
we also think about characters and we&nbsp;
think about people as being broken damaged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.760
and so we can look back and say not only

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:17.040
I know I've limped like this for a long time but&nbsp;
we can ask the question when did I get that limp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:24.960
what was the moment of injury and if we&nbsp;
understand that moment we get a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:29.200
story but we also understand maybe&nbsp;
if this is how it happened with me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.200
this may be how it happens&nbsp;
with my characters as well

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:42.480
and I don't only want to tell bleak stories&nbsp;
about people being injured and damaged I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:48.880
am interested in a credible kind of hope&nbsp;
and so I'm also interested in asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.520
like was there ever a time where I grew&nbsp;
was there ever a time where I healed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:05:00.400
that I overcame a fear what did that moment&nbsp;
look like and if I understand that in myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:10.800
then I'm in a position to portray that in&nbsp;
a character and that will be lifelike and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.640
emotionally authentic because it's rooted in&nbsp;
something that I've actually experienced so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:22.080
I think that that is the vulnerability and&nbsp;
the cost that comes with being a storyteller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:33.840
is that we have to be willing to do that dive&nbsp;
into our our own emotional story and be willing

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:46.000
disguised or not to to bring those stories forward&nbsp;
and it's tough you go into a meeting at a studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:51.600
and you sit with a producer or an executive&nbsp;
and you pitch this character arc and you say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:56.960
I think this is where this character's damage&nbsp;
comes from and what you're really telling is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:05.360
story from your own history you don't necessarily&nbsp;
tell them that but if that gets shot down well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:11.120
that's a little bit vulnerable that's sort of&nbsp;
a rejection of you and your pain and your past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:19.600
but there's also the possibility that the&nbsp;
producer will say oh that's right something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:26.160
like that happened to me and suddenly you've got&nbsp;
a common point of reference for this character

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:36.320
and certainly when you are communicating with&nbsp;
a big audience if those moments actually make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:40.320
it to the screen you've created&nbsp;
the possibility of connection with

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.480
thousands of people millions of people&nbsp;
in a way that they really relate to and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:58.080
when we connect at that deep level with characters&nbsp;
we care we're invested emotionally in what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:04.400
to them that's what gets us sitting on the edge of&nbsp;
our seats it's not just that we've got spectacular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:09.280
visual effects and characters zipping across the&nbsp;
screen if I don't care about those characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:18.160
I'm bored with even the most spectacular&nbsp;
visual effects if I identify with a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:26.800
and deeply care what happens to them then the&nbsp;
tiniest movement either toward or away from danger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:34.960
I feel viscerally so I think that's the&nbsp;
value for writers in connecting with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:41.360
own defining moments so that they can then&nbsp;
enrich their work with the insights they gain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:46.320
some of our comments I've seen&nbsp;
people mention that they didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:50.480
have anything too tragic to write&nbsp;
about but they were still able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:55.920
to portray that in their stories and they said&nbsp;
I didn't come from a dysfunctional family and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:01.040
had a great childhood but there's this myth that&nbsp;
I have to be damaged in order to be a great writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:06.960
do you ever see people that are just too far on&nbsp;
the spectrum either everything is just tragic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.840
and what was me and there was no growth and then&nbsp;
others that now everything was like Disneyland

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:22.240
I do encounter writers a lot who when I ask&nbsp;
them to find moments that have shaped them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:33.200
struggle with that and often discount their life&nbsp;
experience as boring or low stakes and I had I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:44.480
one student who was not a 20 year old who came to&nbsp;
me I knew that she was a pediatrician and but she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:54.640
came to me with this problem of nothing's happened&nbsp;
to me and I said well so your career is you work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.640 --> 00:09:04.240
in the hospital with desperately ill children&nbsp;
and their families I think you have experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:11.200
deeply emotional high stakes things and&nbsp;
she thought about it and she said well

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:17.120
I guess it's true I am a 50 year old&nbsp;
woman and no man has ever loved me

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:28.080
and I said then you you have lots&nbsp;
of stories you have deep emotion but

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:37.840
i think we have to honor our own life&nbsp;
experience and not dismiss it as unimportant

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:51.360
now somebody who only like who wallows in tragedy&nbsp;
and damage I think that we I think that we want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:57.920
more than stories of chaos I think we&nbsp;
do want stories that help us make sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:08.160
of lives that often feel like chaos to us at&nbsp;
least while we're in the middle of the story

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:18.320
and so I really value stories told by writers who&nbsp;
are able to to go out into life and experience it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:26.320
and come back and help make some sense of&nbsp;
it even if it is not the neat Disney package&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:32.640
I think of Joel and Ethan&nbsp;
Coen’s No Country For Old Men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:43.520
and there's not a ton of hope in that movie&nbsp;
there's a line of I think it's Tommy Lee Jones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:50.480
who says you can't stop what's coming referring to&nbsp;
just the sort of growing evil that he was seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:57.920
in the world as a sheriff or marshall in Texas&nbsp;
that's kind of hopeless you can't stop what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:04.720
coming toward the end of the film Tommy Lee&nbsp;
Jones character tells a story of riding out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:10.240
into the wilderness I think it's a dream&nbsp;
he has he's on a horse riding out into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:18.080
wilderness and his dad has ridden out ahead&nbsp;
of him and has lit a campfire and he says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:25.280
in his dream he's riding along and he catches&nbsp;
sight far off in the distance of that campfire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:35.840
and that's it that's all the hope that there is&nbsp;
in that movie but it's enough to say somewhere out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:45.200
there in the darkness there is warmth and there&nbsp;
is light keep writing that can be for somebody who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:53.280
experiences a lot of adversity that can ring&nbsp;
true to them in a way that a Disney movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.840 --> 00:12:02.320
never will that can be credible hope and so I&nbsp;
value that kind of storytelling and I would push a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:11.760
a writer who only sees bleakness to look&nbsp;
harder in the same way that I would encourage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:23.840
a writer who only sees happiness to be more honest&nbsp;
with themselves and own up to whatever the pain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:31.280
and disappointments have been that's hard to do&nbsp;
I think sometimes that can only happen after some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:39.440
therapy and after some years have passed and we&nbsp;
can dare to crack open the package and look inside

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:45.040
but I think the audience craves that kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:53.120
that kind of honesty you're trying to remember&nbsp;
back to Patch Adams it's been a little bit since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:58.320
I've seen it but speaking of a pediatrician&nbsp;
I think at some point it's revealed why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:05.840
the character wants to bring joy to these&nbsp;
children and sort of this clown-esque type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:17.040
yeah I think there's some tragic past which keeps&nbsp;
it from being a silly movie it's not a silly movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:25.360
and it is it's the pain and the&nbsp;
darkness that balances out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:34.320
the fact that he wears a clown nose to clinic&nbsp;
he he knows something about pain and of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:44.480
Robin Williams knew a lot about pain was well&nbsp;
acquainted with that and that undergirded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:51.520
both his dramatic acting and as comic acting.

